@title Seventy-Five Minutes
@kind paper
@source paper:The Effect of Code Obfuscation on Human Program Comprehension
@cite A. H. N. Nguyen†, J. V. Le†, Ilse Lahnstein Coronado, T. N. Nguyen (2026). The Effect of Code Obfuscation on Human Program Comprehension. arXiv:2603.07668 [cs.SE]. † Co-first authors (equal contribution).
@arxiv 2603.07668
@link https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.07668 Read the paper
@link ../../assets/documents/publications/2026SPLASH-SRC.pdf Two-page SRC abstract
@authors A. H. N. Nguyen†, J. V. Le†, Ilse Lahnstein Coronado, T. N. Nguyen
@note The frame story is fiction: the room, the light, the fourth row, the pencil, the order in which the questions arrive, and everything "you" feel. "You" are an invented participant, not a real one. The Clock and the Proctor are abstract roles; no author is voiced and the coauthors did not say these lines. Lines marked §abs quote the paper's arXiv abstract. Lines marked §2 to §3 quote the two-page SPLASH SRC abstract, which describes the tiers and the supervised session; the story assumes it is the same human study. No code from the study is shown.
@status published
@verify
@palette ochre
@include _facts/obfuscation.vn
@cast Clock name="the Clock" hue=38 hairhue=42 skin=1 bob cardigan
@cast Proctor name="the Proctor" hue=205 skin=4 curly masc shirt
@cast You player hue=24

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 1

@chapter 1 The Fourth Row
@transition fade 1200
@bg classroom
@fx dust on
Afternoon. The light comes in low through the windows and lies across the desks like something left behind.
You take a seat in the fourth row. You could not say why the fourth. It is where your feet stopped.
@show Proctor left far
Proctor: Thank you for coming. This is one supervised session: {session_min} minutes, {n_questions} questions.
Proctor: Each question gives you a function and an input. You state the exact output. ^§3.1
Proctor (smile): I cannot answer anything about the code. Think of me as furniture.
You are one of {n_humans_lc} undergraduate CS majors who sit this session.
Above the board there is a clock. You look at it the way you look at a stranger on a platform: briefly, and then away.
@pause 500
@show Clock center far
And then, because it is that kind of afternoon, there is someone sitting under it.
(Nobody else looks up.)
@move Clock right
@show Clock right (neutral)
Clock: {session_min} minutes. I will count them. It is the one thing I am good at.
You: Are you going to hurry me?
@show Clock right (smile) near
@thumb
Clock: No. I only write down how long.
Three things are recorded this afternoon: correctness, response time, and the experience you reported for yourself. ^§abs
She is the second. The third you supplied on the way in.
@hide all
@fx dust off
@fx flash
@flashback on Ten minutes earlier
@transition white 700
@cg page | The form
Before the questions there was a form. Python, and then JavaScript: {n_bands} bands for each, and you place yourself.
It is a strange thing to be asked to weigh yourself. Nobody checks. It is your word.
* Python is home. JavaScript is somewhere you have visited. -> home_py
* JavaScript is home. Python is somewhere you have visited. -> home_js

== home_py
@set home = py
(Whitespace that means something. You tick the higher box for Python and feel faintly loyal.)
-> form_out

== home_js
@set home = js
(Braces, semicolons, the reassurance of punctuation. You tick the higher box for JavaScript and feel faintly loyal.)
-> form_out

== form_out
You hand the form in. It is the last question all day with no right answer.
@pause 400
@cg off
@flashback off
@fx dust on
@show Proctor left (neutral) far
Proctor: You may begin.
@hide Proctor
@pause 400

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 2

@chapter 2 Fast
@transition dissolve 800
@bg classroom
@show Clock right (neutral) far
The first function has had its name taken away. Otherwise it is itself. ^§2.1
You read it the way you read a sign from a moving car. The names go by, you believe them, and they are true.
You write the output. You did not trace anything. You knew, the way you know a friend's handwriting.
Clock (neutral): Quick.
You: Is that bad?
Clock: I do not grade. I count.
@pause 400
The next one has names like the labels on moving boxes that nobody labelled. Short, disjoint identifiers, and nothing to lean on
  but the structure. ^§2.1
It is Python, with the names gone.
You do not decide to slow down. Your finger is already on the first line, moving.
Somewhere a part of you that had been resting has stood up.
@if home == js -> rename_js
(Home, with the furniture taken out. You know where the walls are in the dark.)
-> rename_join

== rename_js
(Not home. And still the shape holds, like a street in a town you stayed in once.)
-> rename_join

== rename_join
You reach the last line and the answer is simply there.
You check the feeling, the way you check a pocket. It was not harder.
(That cannot be right. They took the names away.)
@show Clock right (puzzled) near
Clock: You expected to be slower.
You: I expected it to hurt.
Clock (neutral): I have one column. It does not say what you expected.
You keep it to yourself. It seems like something that happened only to you.
@hide Clock
@pause 500

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 3

@chapter 3 The Trap
@transition dissolve 800
@bg classroom
The light has reached the third row. It will be at yours soon.
The next function has its names back.
They are good names. They agree with one another, as if they had all grown up in the same house. ^§2.1
@transition dissolve 600
@cg screen-code | Illustrative only; the study's functions are not reproduced here
The paper's own example is a variable called `_lastNSecs`. It does not hold seconds. It tracks recursion depth. ^§2.2
An answer arrives before you have finished reading. Whole, confident, already in your hand.
* Write it down. -> trap_fast
* Put the pencil on the first line and trace. -> trap_slow

== trap_fast
@set first = fast
You write it. It takes no time at all.
Then you look at the name again, and at what the name is doing three lines further down.
@fx shake
You draw one line through your answer and start from the top. Slowly, this time.
-> trap_join

== trap_slow
@set first = slow
You trace. The name says one thing and the lines say another, and you hold the name at arm's length all the way down.
It is tiring in a way that reading has not been all afternoon.
-> trap_join

== trap_join
@cg off
@show Clock right (thinking)
Like a Stroop task: the name baits a fast, confident, wrong answer. ^§2.2
@if first == slow -> clock_slow
Clock: Two answers. I only have a place for the time of the second.
You: The names were lying.
Clock (smile): The names were names. You were the one who believed them.
-> clock_join

== clock_slow
Clock: That one took you a while.
You: The names were lying.
Clock (smile): And you read them anyway. Everyone does. You only declined to believe them.
-> clock_join

== clock_join
You cannot tell whether the slow answer is right. You can tell that it is yours.
@hide Clock
@pause 500

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 4

@chapter 4 Slow
@transition dissolve 1200
@bg classroom dusk
@tone dusk
@fx dust off
The light has moved from the desks to the wall. Nobody saw it go.
The next function keeps its names and loses its order. Dispatchers and indirect jumps: what is written next is no longer
  what happens next. ^§2.1
So you become the machine. One step, one state, one step. The margin of your sheet fills with small numbers.
The fast kind of reading is over for the afternoon. Nobody announced it.
@transition dissolve 700
@cg page | The margin
And then one that has lost both, names and order together. The two-page abstract calls it an upper bound on difficulty. ^§2.1
You read it. You read it again.
You find you have been looking at the same line for some time, and that it has not been looking back.
@cg off
@show Clock right (worried) far
Clock: You have been here a long while.
@show Clock right (worried) near
Clock: I am not hurrying you. I said I would not. I am only telling you what I have written down.
* Stay with it. -> stay
* Write your best answer and turn the page. -> leave

== stay
You stay. At some point staying stops being thinking and becomes only staying. You notice too late to say when.
-> slow_join

== leave
You write something. It is less an answer than a way of leaving a room without slamming the door.
-> slow_join

== slow_join
Clock (thinking): There is a slow that is care and a slow that is being lost. From where I sit they are the same number.
You: Which was mine?
Clock (neutral): I count. I told you.
@move Clock center
The rest go by like weather. Some you trace. Some you simply know. You stop keeping track of which.
@hide Clock
@pause 600

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 5

@chapter 5 Pencils Down
@transition dissolve 1000
@bg classroom dusk
@tone dusk
@show Proctor left (neutral)
Proctor: Time. Pencils down, please.
Your sheet goes on the pile. {n_answers} responses in all, each one paired with whether it was right and how long it took.
@show Clock right (neutral) far
You: Can you tell me how I did?
Proctor (worried): I cannot. I am sorry.
You: Can you tell me what it was about?
Proctor (smile): When it is written.
@hide all
@transition dissolve 1000
@cg two-chairs | Afterwards
The room empties the way rooms do, all at once and then for a long time.
Later, it is written. This is what it says.
@mode nvl
Obfuscation generally increases the time required to reason about code, and tends to reduce prediction accuracy. ^§abs
But the relationship between obfuscation strength and performance is not strictly monotonic, and it varies across programming languages. ^§abs
JavaScript exhibits the expected pattern of increasing difficulty with stronger obfuscation. ^§abs
Python displays a more complex trend: certain renaming transformations can perform comparably to, or occasionally better than,
  the unobfuscated baseline. ^§abs
(So it did not happen only to you.)
@page
Response times suggest that obfuscation shifts participants away from rapid, heuristic reasoning toward slower and more
  deliberate reasoning. ^§abs
Performance appears highest within a moderate range of response times. Careful deliberation can improve accuracy;
  extremely long response times often correspond to confusion. ^§abs
(Care, and being lost. The abstract has a clause for each. It still cannot say which one was yours.)
@page
Programming experience predicts performance primarily within a given language, with limited transfer across languages. ^§abs
Which suggests that obfuscation challenges language-specific familiarity more than general programming ability. ^§abs
(The box you ticked for home was a promise about home. It never said anything about the other place.)
@mode adv
@cg off
@transition dissolve 900
@bg corridor dusk
@show Clock center far
Clock (smile): I stopped when you put the pencil down. The rest is not mine to count.
@hide Clock
@transition dissolve 900
@cg corridor-light | The way out
You walk out into a corridor the colour of the end of the day.
For the first time since you sat down, nobody is writing down how long you take.
@pause 700
@tone none
@end
